Do not report a content scale factor to a hosted VST3 on macOS.

IPlugViewContentScaleSupport is a Windows/Linux interface: on macOS a plug-in
view's coordinates are already logical points, and the plug-in reads the backing
scale from its own NSWindow. JUCE 8.0.14 calls setContentScaleFactor() there
anyway - with 1.0, since the macOS peer reports no platform scale - and only
suppresses the resulting failure warning. That is harmless for a plug-in which
rejects the call, but a plug-in that *accepts* it throws away the retina scale
it had correctly taken from the screen: every iPlug2 build before the OS_WIN
guard (Neural Amp Modeler among them) then renders its UI at 1x into the
top-left quarter of its 2x layer, leaving the rest of the window undrawn.

So on macOS, behave as a host with no scale interface at all: adopt the view's
own size and report nothing. Windows and Linux keep JUCE's behaviour verbatim.

diff --git a/modules/juce_audio_processors/format_types/juce_VST3PluginFormat.cpp b/modules/juce_audio_processors/format_types/juce_VST3PluginFormat.cpp
--- a/modules/juce_audio_processors/format_types/juce_VST3PluginFormat.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_audio_processors/format_types/juce_VST3PluginFormat.cpp
@@ -498,32 +498,47 @@
 
     void updatePluginScale()
     {
+       #if JUCE_MAC
+        // PLECTRIFY_NO_MAC_CONTENT_SCALE: nothing to report here, so this
+        // is the no-scale-interface path - see setContentScaleFactor().
+        resizeToFit();
+       #else
         if (scaleInterface != nullptr)
             setContentScaleFactor();
         else
             resizeToFit();
+       #endif
     }
 
     void setContentScaleFactor()
     {
+       #if ! JUCE_MAC
         if (scaleInterface != nullptr)
         {
             [[maybe_unused]] const auto result = scaleInterface->setContentScaleFactor ((IPlugViewContentScaleSupport::ScaleFactor) getEffectiveScale());
-
-           #if ! JUCE_MAC
             [[maybe_unused]] const auto warning = warnOnFailure (result);
-           #endif
         }
+       #else
+        // PLECTRIFY_NO_MAC_CONTENT_SCALE: IPlugViewContentScaleSupport is a
+        // Windows/Linux interface. A macOS view is measured in logical
+        // points already and the plug-in takes the backing scale from its
+        // own NSWindow, so there is no scale for a host to report - and a
+        // plug-in that accepts one regardless (every iPlug2 build before
+        // the OS_WIN guard) replaces its real 2x screen scale with our
+        // 1.0 and then draws at half size into a quarter of its layer.
+       #endif
     }
 
     void setScaleFactor (float s) override
     {
         userScaleFactor = s;
         setContentScaleFactor();
         resizeToFit();
     }
 
+    // Unused on macOS, where nothing is reported to the plug-in.
+    [[maybe_unused]]
     float getEffectiveScale() const
     {
         return nativeScaleFactor * userScaleFactor;
     }
